analysis:
There are 7 failures in total. Two categories are tied as the largest: rejected_by_verifier (3) and not_retrieved (3). The remaining one is false_positive_earlier.

**Rejected by the verifier.** In all three cases the true quotation is a clean paraphrase of the claim. It covers every component and adds nothing of substance. The paraphrase differences are only these:
- a euphemism has been spelled out;
- a conditional "if X then Y" has been restated as "X means Y";
- a repeated "I don't think" has been merged into one clause;
- first person has become third person;
- separate sentences have been joined with "adding that".

The current prompt's strict Extent and framing rules (wording such as "materially different framing" and "closely tracks") were added to cut false positives. At low effort they now seem to make the verifier reject clean paraphrases that share few words with the claim. Raising effort to medium was already tried and only gained +2, below the threshold of 3. A prompt fix is cheaper: keep the strictness about extra content and missing components, but say clearly that differences in wording alone are never grounds to reject. Calls per claim stay the same.

**Not retrieved.** All three of these claims are heavily reworded with synonyms (for example "hopeful" vs "optimistic", "grave" vs "serious", "peril" vs "danger"), so BM25 overlap is low. Raising k to 60 with batch 30 has been pre-screened positive four times (+3, +2, +1, +1) but never fully evaluated. It keeps calls per claim at 2. The surname speaker filter is untested; if it is applied before the top-k cut, it would put the right speaker's quotes on the candidate list and also cut verifier calls.

**False positive earlier.** This is a single case: two longer versions of the quote that make stronger claims were matched. The Extent rule in the proposed prompt is kept, so this should not get worse.
